1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,605
Two pencils
feel like one point?

2
00:00:04,655 --> 00:00:06,675
Hold two pencils side by side,
blunt ends down

3
00:00:06,725 --> 00:00:08,525
Your friend closes their eyes

4
00:00:08,575 --> 00:00:10,475
Press lightly and ask
"One or two?"

5
00:00:10,525 --> 00:00:13,845
Never use the sharp ends!

6
00:00:13,895 --> 00:00:16,845
First, lightly on a fingertip

7
00:00:16,895 --> 00:00:18,845
"Two!" Two points are felt

8
00:00:18,895 --> 00:00:21,845
Now the forearm, the same way

9
00:00:21,895 --> 00:00:25,845
Huh? It feels like just one!

10
00:00:25,895 --> 00:00:29,415
Same two pencils!

11
00:00:29,465 --> 00:00:31,785
Touch-sensing cells in your skin
each take care of their own patch

12
00:00:31,835 --> 00:00:34,285
Fingertips are packed tight,
so the two points hit different cells

13
00:00:34,335 --> 00:00:36,785
The forearm is sparse,
so both points land on one cell

14
00:00:36,835 --> 00:00:40,555
One cell sends one signal
→ the brain feels "one point"

15
00:00:40,605 --> 00:00:44,725
That's why fingertips
can read tiny braille dots

16
00:00:44,775 --> 00:00:47,175
What about the back of the hand, cheek, or sole?

17
00:00:47,225 --> 00:00:49,675
NEXT: The same water
feels cold AND warm?
